//For every element, I compare it with the element immediately before it.

If the current element is already greater than or equal to the previous element, nothing needs to be done.
If the current element is smaller than the previous element, I have no choice but to increase the current element until it becomes equal to the previous element.

The number of operations needed is simply:

previous value − current value

After increasing the current element, I treat its new value as the previous value for the next element.
public class Main
{
	public static void main(String[] args) {
	   // Scanner sc=new Scanner(System.in);
	    
	    int []a={5,2,2,3};
	    int prev=0;
	    int curr=1;int op=0;
	    while(curr<a.length){
	    if(a[curr]<a[prev]){
	        op+=a[prev]-a[curr];
	        a[curr]=a[prev];
	    }
	    prev++;
	    curr++;
	    
	    }
		System.out.println(op);
	}
}
